---
/**
 * Calm CPU vs GPU comparison: few complex workers vs many parallel simple ops.
 */
---

<figure class="diagram diagram--anim">
  <figcaption class="diagram__caption">
    <span class="mono-label">Diagram</span>
    CPU vs GPU — different strengths, same computer story
  </figcaption>
  <div class="diagram__frame">
    <svg
      class="diagram__svg"
      viewBox="0 0 620 280"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="gpu-title gpu-desc"
    >
      <title id="gpu-title">CPU versus GPU parallelism</title>
      <desc id="gpu-desc">
        Left: a few large CPU cores handling complex tasks one after another. Right: many small GPU
        cores doing simple multiply-add work in parallel. Labels note orchestration versus matrix math.
      </desc>

      <rect x="12" y="12" width="596" height="256" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-quiet)" stroke="var(--rule)" />

      <!-- CPU panel -->
      <text class="diagram-label" x="120" y="44" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--brand)" font-weight="600">CPU</text>
      <text class="diagram-hint" x="120" y="64" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Few cores · complex tasks</text>

      <g class="cpu-core">
        <rect x="48" y="88" width="144" height="44" rx="3" fill="var(--mist-soft)" stroke="var(--brand)" />
        <text x="120" y="115" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-size="13">Core A</text>
      </g>
      <g class="cpu-core" style="animation-delay: 0.9s">
        <rect x="48" y="148" width="144" height="44" rx="3" fill="var(--mist-soft)" stroke="var(--brand)" />
        <text x="120" y="175" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-size="13">Core B</text>
      </g>
      <text class="diagram-hint" x="120" y="230" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Logic, branching,</text>
      <text class="diagram-hint" x="120" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">orchestration</text>

      <!-- divider -->
      <line x1="310" y1="36" x2="310" y2="252" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 6" />

      <!-- GPU panel -->
      <text class="diagram-label" x="460" y="44" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--action)" font-weight="600">GPU</text>
      <text class="diagram-hint" x="460" y="64" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Many cores · parallel ops</text>

      {
        [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((row) =>
          [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((col) => {
            const x = 368 + col * 28;
            const y = 84 + row * 22;
            const delay = ((row * 6 + col) % 12) * 0.12;
            return (
              <rect
                class="gpu-cell"
                x={x}
                y={y}
                width="22"
                height="16"
                rx="2"
                fill="var(--cobalt-soft)"
                stroke="var(--action)"
                stroke-opacity="0.45"
                style={`animation-delay: ${delay}s`}
              />
            );
          }),
        )
      }

      <text class="diagram-hint" x="460" y="230" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Matrix math, batches,</text>
      <text class="diagram-hint" x="460" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">attention at scale</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <p class="diagram__legend">
    Training and large-model inference flood the chip with similar multiply-adds — GPUs (and other
    accelerators) are built for that shape of work.
  </p>
</figure>

<style>
  .cpu-core {
    animation: cpu-pulse 2.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  .gpu-cell {
    animation: gpu-twinkle 2.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  @keyframes cpu-pulse {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }
  @keyframes gpu-twinkle {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.45;
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cpu-core,
    .gpu-cell {
      animation: none !important;
      opacity: 0.9;
    }
  }
</style>
